<div class="hebrew-block">וְזֶה בְּחִינַת נְדָרִים, כִּי בֶּאֱמֶת הַמִּצְוָה הַזֹּאת שֶׁל נְדָרִים הִוא פְּלִיאָה גְּדוֹלָה שֶׁיֵּשׁ כֹּחַ בְּיַד אִישׁ הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי לֶאֱסֹר מַה שֶּׁהִתִּירָה תּוֹרָה. וּמֵאַחַר שֶׁהַתּוֹרָה הִתִּירָה הַדָּבָר מֵהֵיכָן יִהְיֶה כֹּחַ לְהָאָדָם לֶאֱסֹר הַדָּבָר עַל עַצְמוֹ. וְעַל-כֵּן בֶּאֱמֶת נִקְרָאִים הַנְּדָרִים פְּלִיאוֹת חָכְמָה, בְּחִינַת אִישׁ כִּי יַפְלִא לִנְדֹּר נֶדֶר וְכוּ' וְכַמּוּבָא. אַךְ הָעִנְיָן עַל-פִּי הַנַּ"ל, כִּי בְּכָל אָדָם מִיִּשְׂרָאֵל יֵשׁ חֵלֶק אֱלוֹקַ מִמַּעַל. וְעִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת הוּא בְּלֵב הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי וּמֵחֲמַת אוֹר הָאֱלֹקוּת שֶׁבְּלִבּוֹ בְּחִינַת צוּר לְבָבִי. עַל-כֵּן מִשְׁתּוֹקֵק תָּמִיד לַה' יִתְבָּרַךְ עַד אֵין סוֹף וְאֵין תַּכְלִית וּמֵחֲמַת זֶה אוֹר לֶהָבִיּוּת אִישׁ הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי הוּא בְּחִינַת אֵין סוֹף, כִּי אֵין סוֹף וְאֵין תַּכְלִית לִתְשׁוּקָתוֹ וְכַנַּ"ל. וְהִנֵּה לְפִי עֹצֶם אוֹר לֶהָבִיּוּתוֹ עַד אֵין סוֹף אֵינוֹ מַסְפִּיק לוֹ תַּרְיַ"ג מִצְוֹת הַתּוֹרָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה, כִּי אוֹר לֶהָבִיּוּתוֹ הוּא נִכְסָף וּמִשְׁתּוֹקֵק וּמִתְגַּעְגֵּעַ לִהְיוֹת דָּבוּק וְנִכְלָל בַּה' יִתְבָּרַךְ תָּמִיד לְעוֹלָם וָעֶד וְשֶׁלֹּא יְפַנֶּה עַצְמוֹ לְשׁוּם דָּבָר מֵהָעוֹלָם אֲפִלּוּ לְשׁוּם דָּבָר הַמֻּתָּר רַק יִהְיֶה תָּמִיד דָּבוּק בַּה' יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּלִי הֶפְסֵק רֶגַע כְּלָל. אַךְ בֶּאֱמֶת זֶה אִי אֶפְשָׁר בְּשׁוּם אֹפֶן, כִּי אִם כֵּן הָיָה מִתְבַּטֵּל בִּמְצִיאוּת וְלֹא הָיָה מִתְקַיֵּם יְשׁוּתוֹ כְּלָל וְהָיָה נֶחֱרָב הָעוֹלָם חַס וְשָׁלוֹם, וְה' יִתְבָּרַךְ רוֹצֶה בְּקִיּוּם הָעוֹלָם. עַל-כֵּן צְרִיכִין בְּהֶכְרֵחַ לְצַמְצֵם אוֹר הַהִתְלַהֲבוּת הַזֹּאת וְלַעֲבֹד אֶת ה' יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּהַדְרָגָה וּבְמִדָּה. וְזֶה בְּחִינַת כְּלַל כָּל הַתּוֹרָה כֻּלָּהּ אֲשֶׁר נִזְהַרְנוּ לִבְלִי לָסוּר מִדֶּרֶךְ הַתּוֹרָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל, כִּי אֲפִלּוּ לְיָמִין אָסוּר לָסוּר מִדֶּרֶךְ הַיָּשָׁר שֶׁל הַתּוֹרָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה, הַיְנוּ לְהַרְבּוֹת וּלְהוֹסִיף יוֹתֵר מִמִּצְוֹת הַתּוֹרָה שֶׁזֶּה בְּחִינַת נוֹטֶה לְיָמִין אָסוּר לָנוּ גַּם כֵּן, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב, לֹא תוֹסִיפוּ עַל הַדָּבָר וְלֹא תִגְרְעוּ מִמֶּנּוּ, כִּי לְפִי עֹצֶם לֶהָבִיּוּתוֹ שֶׁל אִישׁ הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי הָיָה נִכְסָף לְהוֹסִיף עַל מִצְוַת הַתּוֹרָה. אַךְ הַתּוֹרָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה הִזְהִירָה עַל זֹאת בְּלָאו דְּבַל תּוֹסִיף, כִּי כְּשֶׁמּוֹסִיפִין עַל הַתּוֹרָה אֲפִלּוּ אִם כַּוָּנָתוֹ לַשָּׁמַיִם אַף-עַל-פִּי-כֵן הוּא נוֹטֶה מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ הַיָּשָׁר וּמְקַלְקֵל הַצִּמְצוּם הַקָּדוֹשׁ שֶׁזֶּה עִקַּר קִיּוּם הָעוֹלָמוֹת, וַאֲזַי, הוּא בְּחִינַת אֵין סוֹף, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת רִבּוּי אוֹר, שֶׁגּוֹרֵם חַס וְשָׁלוֹם, שְׁבִירַת כֵּלִים חֻרְבַּן הָעוֹלָמוֹת: וְגַם עִקַּר הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע וְכָל הַתַּאֲוֹת וְכָל הַחֲטָאִים חַס וְשָׁלוֹם בָּאִין מִזֶּה, כִּי יָדוּעַ שֶׁכָּל הַקְּלִפּוֹת, שֶׁהֵם הַתַּאֲוֹת וְהַחֲטָאִים, כֻּלָּם בָּאִים מִשְּׁבִירַת כֵּלִים שֶׁהָיָה עַל-יְדֵי רִבּוּי אוֹר כַּיָּדוּעַ. וְזֶה בְּחִינַת יַעֲלוּ שָׁמַיִם יֵרְדוּ תְּהֹמֹת ((תְּהִלִּים קז) שֶׁלִּפְעָמִים הָאָדָם בּוֹעֵר לַה' יִתְבָּרַךְ יוֹתֵר מִמַּדְרֵגָתוֹ שֶׁזֶּה בְּחִינַת יַעֲלוּ שָׁמַיִם. וּכְשֶׁאֵינוֹ זוֹכֶה לְצַמְצֵם הָאוֹר לְפִי מַדְרֵגָתוֹ עַל-יְדֵי-זֶה יֵרְדוּ תְּהֹמֹת חַס וְשָׁלוֹם, כִּי עַל-יְדֵי-זֶה נוֹפֵל אַחַר כָּךְ חַס וְשָׁלוֹם מְאֹד, כִּי זֶה בְּחִינַת רִבּוּי אוֹר וְכוּ' כַּנַּ"ל. וְעַל-כֵּן עַל-פִּי רֹב הָאָדָם נוֹפֵל לַעֲוֹנוֹת עַל עַל-יְדֵי רִבּוּי הַמִּצְוֹת וְהַדִּקְדּוּקִים שֶׁל הַבַּעַל דָּבָר שֶׁמַּרְבֶּה לְהַחְמִיר וּלְדַקְדֵּק עָלָיו בְּיוֹתֵר וּמְפַתֵּהוּ לַמִּצְוֹת שֶׁלּוֹ וּמִתְלַבֵּשׁ עַצְמוֹ בְּמִצְוֹת וְאַחַר כָּךְ מְבִיאוֹ לִידֵי עֲוֹנוֹת חַס וְשָׁלוֹם. כִּי רִבּוּי הַחֻמְרוֹת וְהַדִּקְדּוּקִים זֶה בְּחִינַת רִבּוּי אוֹר שֶׁגּוֹרֵם שְׁבִירַת כֵּלִים חַס וְשָׁלוֹם כַּנַּ"ל שֶׁמִּשָּׁם נִמְשָׁכִין כָּל הָעֲוֹנוֹת חַס וְשָׁלוֹם כַּנַּ"ל. וְזֶה בְּחִינַת אַל תִּצְדַּק הַרְבֵּה וְאַל תִּרְשַׁע הַרְבֵּה ((קֹהֶלֶת ז) כִּי הָא בְּהָא תַּלְיָא שֶׁצָּרִיךְ לִזָּהֵר שֶׁלֹּא יִצְדַּק הַרְבֵּה כְּדֵי שֶׁלֹּא יִרְשַׁע הַרְבֵּה חַס וְשָׁלוֹם, עַל-יְדֵי-זֶה כַּנַּ"ל:</div>

<p class="first">And this is the aspect of <strong><em>nedarim</em></strong>. For in truth, this <em>mitzvah</em> of <em>nedarim</em> is a great wonder — that an Israelite person has the power to forbid that which the Torah permitted. And since the Torah permitted the matter, from where does a person have the power to forbid the matter upon himself? Therefore, in truth, <em>nedarim</em> are called <span class="key-term">pelios chochmah</span> <span class="added">[wonders of wisdom]</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"When a man shall express a wonder, to vow a vow"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Numbers 6:2)</span>, <span class="added">[where the word for "express" (<em>yafli</em>) is related to <em>peleh</em> — wonder]</span>, as is brought.</p>

<p>However, the explanation is according to the above. For in every Israelite person there is <span class="verse-inline">"a portion of G-d from above"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Job 31:2)</span>. And the essence of the G-dliness is in the heart of the Israelite, and because of the light of G-dliness in his heart — the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"Rock of my heart"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Psalms 73:26)</span> — therefore he longs constantly for Hashem without end and without limit. And because of this, the fiery light of the Israelite person is the aspect of <em>ain sof</em>, for there is no end and no limit to his longing, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And behold, according to the intensity of his fiery light which is without end, the 613 <em>mitzvos</em> of the holy Torah are not sufficient for him. For the light of his <em>hislahavus</em> yearns and longs and pines to be attached and included in Hashem always, forever and ever, and that he should not turn himself to any worldly thing at all — not even to any permitted thing — but that he should always be attached to Hashem without a moment's pause whatsoever. But in truth, this is impossible in any way, for if so, he would become nullified from existence and his selfhood would not endure at all, and the world would be destroyed, G-d forbid — and Hashem desires the continued existence of the world.</p>

<p>Therefore one must necessarily contract this fiery light and serve Hashem in gradual stages and in measured proportion. And this is the aspect of the general principle of the entire Torah — that we are cautioned not to deviate from the path of the holy Torah, to the right or to the left. For <strong>even to the right</strong> it is forbidden to deviate from the straight path of the holy Torah — that is, to add and increase beyond the <em>mitzvos</em> of the Torah. This, the aspect of veering to the right, is also forbidden to us, as it is written, <span class="verse-inline">"You shall not add to the matter, and you shall not subtract from it"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Deuteronomy 4:2)</span>. For according to the intense <em>hislahavus</em> of the Israelite person, he would yearn to add upon the <em>mitzvos</em> of the Torah. But the holy Torah warned about this with the prohibition of <em>bal tosif</em> <span class="added">[do not add]</span>. For when one adds upon the Torah — even if his intention is for the sake of Heaven — nevertheless he deviates from the straight path and damages the holy <em>tzimtzum</em>, which is the primary sustenance of the worlds. And then it is the aspect of <em>ain sof</em> — the aspect of <strong>excess light</strong> (<span class="key-term">ribui or</span>) — which causes, G-d forbid, <em>sheviras hakailim</em> <span class="added">[the shattering of the vessels]</span>, the destruction of the worlds.</p>

<p>And also, the primary <em>yetzer hara</em> <span class="added">[evil inclination]</span>, and all the desires, and all the sins, G-d forbid, come from this. For it is known that all the <em>klipos</em> — which are the desires and the sins — all come from <em>sheviras hakailim</em>, which occurred through excess light, as is known. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"They ascend to the heavens, they descend to the depths"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Psalms 107:26)</span> — for sometimes a person burns for Hashem beyond his level, and this is the aspect of "they ascend to the heavens." And when he does not merit contracting the light according to his level, through this "they descend to the depths," G-d forbid — for through this he falls afterwards greatly, G-d forbid, for this is the aspect of excess light, etc., as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And therefore, <strong>most often a person falls into sins through the excess <em>mitzvos</em> and stringencies of the <em>ba'al davar</em></strong> <span class="added">[the Adversary]</span>, who increases stringencies and demands upon him excessively, and entices him with <em>mitzvos</em> of his own <span class="added">[devising]</span>, and clothes himself in <em>mitzvos</em> — and afterwards brings him to sins, G-d forbid. For the excess of stringencies and demands — this is the aspect of excess light that causes <em>sheviras hakailim</em>, G-d forbid, as mentioned above, from which all the sins are drawn, G-d forbid, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"Do not be overly righteous, and do not be overly wicked"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Ecclesiastes 7:16)</span> — for the one depends on the other: one must be careful not to be overly righteous, so that he does not become overly wicked, G-d forbid, through this, as mentioned above.</p>

