<div class="hebrew-block">וְזֶה שֶׁמּוּבָא בְּהַמַּעֲשִׂיּוֹת שֶׁל רַבֵּנוּ זַ"ל בְּהַמַּעֲשֶׂה שֶׁל הַמֶּלֶךְ שֶׁגָּזַר גֵּרוּשׁ בִּגְזֵרַת שְׁמָד, עַל-יְדֵי צִיצִית וּתְפִלִּין יְכוֹלִין לַעֲבֹר בְּתוֹךְ הָאֵשׁ הַגְּדוֹלָה. כִּי הָאֵשׁ הַגְּדוֹלָה, נִמְשֶׁכֶת וְנִשְׁתַּלְשְׁלָה מֵאֵשׁ שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה מִבְּחִינַת כִּי ה' אֱלֹקֶיךָ אֵשׁ אֹכְלָה הוּא, מִבְּחִינַת אוֹר הַהִתְלַהֲבוּת שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת אֵין סוֹף וְאֵשׁ הַזֹּאת הִוא בְּחִינַת תְּכֵלֶת דְּאָכְלָא כֹּלָּא וְשֵׁיצֵי כֻּלָּא. אֲבָל יִשְׂרָאֵל הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה' מְקַבְּלִים חִיּוּתָם מִמֶּנּוּ וְכוּ', כַּמְבֹאָר שָׁם בַּמַּאֲמָר הַנַּ"ל. כִּי יִשְׂרָאֵל עַל-יְדֵי מִצְוַת צִיצִית וּתְפִלִּין הֵם מְצַמְצְמִין הָאוֹר כַּנַּ"ל, כִּי צִיצִית מְבֹאָר בַּמַּאֲמָר הַנַּ"ל, שֶׁהִוא בְּחִינַת צִמְצוּם הַקָּדוֹשׁ הַנַּ"ל. וּתְפִלִּין הֵם בְּחִינַת חוֹתָם הַנַּ"ל. עַל-כֵּן צִיצִית וּתְפִלִּין הֵם מְצַמְצְמִין הָאוֹר כַּנַּ"ל. וְעַל-כֵּן יְכוֹלִים לַעֲבֹר בְּתוֹךְ הָאֵשׁ עַל-יְדֵי צִיצִית וּתְפִלִּין. כִּי אַדְּרַבָּא הֵם מְקַבְּלִין חִיּוּת מִשָּׁם בִּבְחִינַת וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה' אֱלֹקֵיכֶם חַיִּים כֻּלְּכֶם הַיּוֹם אָמֵן וְאָמֵן: שַׁיָּךְ לְעֵיל יד: וְזֶהוּ שֶׁסִּיֵּם, אִם יִתֵּן אִישׁ אֶת כָּל הוֹן בֵּיתוֹ בָּאַהֲבָה בּוֹז יָבוּזוּ לוֹ. כִּי כְּשֶׁזּוֹכִין לַחוֹתָמוֹת הַנַּ"ל, דְּהַיְנוּ לְצַמְצֵם אוֹר הָאֵין סוֹף בְּהַדְרָגָה וּבְמִדָּה כַּנַּ"ל, עַל-יְדֵי-זֶה זוֹכִין לְאוֹרָיְתָא דְּעַתִּיקָא סְתִימָאָה שֶׁהִיא כֹּלָּא יְמִינָא וְלֵית שְׂמֹאל וְלֵית עֹשֶׁר תַּמָּן וְכוּ', כַּמְבֹאָר כָּל זֶה הֵיטֵב בַּמַּאֲמָר הַנַּ"ל. וְזֶהוּ שִׂימֵנִי כַחוֹתָם וְכוּ', הַיְנוּ צִמְצוּם הָאוֹר בְּהַדְרָגָה וּבְמִדָּה בִּבְחִינַת חוֹתָם כַּנַּ"ל. כִּי עַזָּה כַמָּוֶת אַהֲבָה וְכוּ' רְשָׁפֶיהָ רִשְׁפֵּי אֵשׁ שַׁלְהֶבֶתיָהּ, דְּהַיְנוּ שֶׁהַלֵּב בּוֹעֵר עַד אֵין סוֹף וְעַל-כֵּן צְרִיכִין לַצִּמְצוּם הַקָּדוֹשׁ בְּחִינַת חוֹתָמוֹת הַנַּ"ל. וַאֲזַי אִם יִתֵּן אִישׁ אֶת כָּל הוֹן בֵּיתוֹ בָּאַהֲבָה בּוֹז יָבוּזוּ לוֹ. כִּי אָז עַל-יְדֵי בְּחִינַת חוֹתָם הַנַּ"ל זוֹכִין לְאוֹרָיְתָא דְּעַתִּיקָא סְתִימָאָה שֶׁאָז מְבַזִּין כָּל הוֹן דְּעָלְמָא, כִּי לֵית שְׂמֹאל וְלֵית עֹשֶׁר תַּמָּן, שֶׁזֶּהוּ בְּחִינַת וּנְוַת בַּיִת תְּחַלֵּק שָׁלָל, שֶׁמְּבַזִּין אֶת הַמָּעוֹת, בְּחִינַת בּוֹז יָבוּזוּ לוֹ, עַיֵּן שָׁם בְּהַמַּאֲמָר הַנַּ"ל בְּסוֹפוֹ:</div>

<p class="first">And this is what is brought in the <strong>stories of our Rebbe</strong> of blessed memory, in the story of the king who decreed an expulsion with a decree of forced conversion: that through <em>tzitzis</em> and tefillin one can pass through the great fire. For the great fire is drawn down and cascades from the supernal fire, from the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"for Hashem your G-d is a consuming fire"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Deuteronomy 4:24)</span> — from the aspect of the fiery light of <em>hislahavus</em>, which is the aspect of <em>ain sof</em>. And this fire is the aspect of <span class="key-term">techailes d'achla kola v'shaitzay kula</span> <span class="added">[the blue/consuming-fire that devours all and destroys all]</span>. But Israel, who are attached to Hashem, receive their life-force from Him, etc. — as is explained there in the aforementioned discourse.</p>

<p>For Israel, through the <em>mitzvah</em> of <em>tzitzis</em> and tefillin, contract the light, as mentioned above. For <em>tzitzis</em> is explained in the aforementioned discourse as the aspect of the aforementioned holy <em>tzimtzum</em>. And tefillin are the aspect of the aforementioned <em>chosam</em>. Therefore <em>tzitzis</em> and tefillin contract the light, as mentioned above. And therefore one can pass through the fire through <em>tzitzis</em> and tefillin — for on the contrary, they receive life-force from there, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"And you who are attached to Hashem your G-d are all alive today"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Deuteronomy 4:4)</span>. <em>Amen</em> and <em>Amen</em>.</p>

<p><span class="added">[The following belongs to section 11 above:]</span> And this is the meaning of the concluding verse: <span class="verse-inline">"If a man would give all the wealth of his house for love — they would utterly scorn him"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Song of Songs 8:7)</span>. For when one merits the aforementioned <em>chosamos</em> <span class="added">[seals]</span> — that is, to contract the <em>or ain sof</em> in gradual stages and in measured proportion, as mentioned above — through this one merits <span class="key-term">Oraysa d'Asika Sesima'ah</span> <span class="added">[the Torah of the Hidden Ancient One]</span>, which is entirely right <span class="added">[<em>chesed</em>]</span>, and there is no left there and no wealth there, etc., as all this is explained well in the aforementioned discourse. And this is <span class="verse-inline">"Set me as a seal…"</span> etc. — that is, the contraction of the light in gradual stages and in measured proportion, in the aspect of a <em>chosam</em>, as mentioned above. <span class="verse-inline">"For love is as strong as death… its flames are flames of fire, a blaze of G-d"</span> — that is, the heart burns without end, and therefore one needs the holy <em>tzimtzum</em>, the aspect of the aforementioned <em>chosamos</em>. And then, <span class="verse-inline">"If a man would give all the wealth of his house for love — they would utterly scorn him"</span> — for then, through the aspect of the aforementioned <em>chosam</em>, one merits the Torah of the Hidden Ancient One. And then they scorn all the wealth of the world, for there is no left there and no wealth there. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"and the virtuous woman of the house divides the spoil"</span> <span class="added">[<em>unvas bayis techalek shalal</em>]</span> — that they scorn the money, the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"they would utterly scorn him"</span> — see there in the aforementioned discourse at its conclusion.</p>

<hr>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Translator's Summary</h3>

<p>This halacha, based on Likutay Moharan Torah 49 ("He placed a tent for the sun"), develops a comprehensive kabbalistic psychology of spiritual desire and restraint. The central metaphor is the <em>tzimtzum</em>: just as Hashem contracted His infinite light to create a vacant space (<em>chalal panui</em>) in which the worlds could exist, so must the Israelite contract the infinite fire of his longing for G-d in order to create vessels (<em>kailim</em>) capable of receiving that light in measured proportion.</p>

<p><strong>Core Paradox:</strong> The heart of the Jew burns with infinite longing for Hashem (<em>or hislahavus</em> = <em>ain sof</em>). This is genuine and holy — yet if uncontracted, it becomes "excess light" (<em>ribui or</em>) that shatters vessels, the same dynamic that produced the primordial <em>sheviras hakailim</em> and underlies all sin. Even the sin of Adam HaRishon was rooted in addition — Chavah adding "you shall not touch it" — and the Serpent's strategy was to incite excess piety. The first Tablets, given with thunderous revelation, shattered; the second, given in silence (<em>tzimtzum</em>), endured.</p>

<p><strong>Nedarim as Holy Tzimtzum:</strong> The <em>neder</em> is the mechanism by which the infinite desire of the heart (<em>Binah</em> / <em>ain sof</em>) is contracted into the finite speech of the mouth (<em>kailim</em> / <em>midos</em>). Uttering the vow transforms boundless longing into a specific, fulfillable commandment — actual Torah. A vow on "all fruits in the world" is void because it is <em>ain sof</em> without <em>tzimtzum</em>. Vows are especially a <em>mitzvah</em> for teshuvah (penitence), creating new vessels to replace damaged channels.</p>

<p><strong>Annulment:</strong> The <em>chacham</em> uproots the vow by ascending to the <em>ain sof</em> (where all contractions are nullified in their root) and drawing new contractions through his mouth ("mutar lach"). Three laymen can annul because the Torah's <em>tzimtzum</em> is threefold: Chesed/Kohain/Avraham (infinite light), Gevurah/Levi/Yitzchak (contraction), and the measured drawing-in (Yisra'ail/Ya'akov). Father and husband annul because their higher level makes the vower's <em>tzimtzum</em> as <em>ain sof</em> relative to them.</p>

<p><strong>Song of Songs and Tefillin:</strong> "Set me as a seal (<em>chosam</em>) upon Your heart" — the <em>chosam</em> is the <em>tzimtzum</em> itself: tefillin of the head (<em>baisa ila'ah</em> / Binah) and of the hand (<em>baisa tata'ah</em> / Malchus). Knots, housings, sinews, and hair are all <em>tzimtzumim</em> to contain the infinite <em>mochin</em>. Through <em>tzitzis</em> and tefillin one can pass through fire — for they contract the consuming blaze into life-giving attachment.</p>

